+### Scene 3 · 1:03–1:39 · Locked evaluation, unusable output
+<!-- claim_id: 02-c2 -->
 
 **Narration**
 
-The production-scale study reports a peak offline Recall at six result of one point eight two times the hand-tuned baseline. Across evaluation dates, the mean supports one point six three times the baseline. Preserve both qualifications: peak and offline. Components were added sequentially, so these comparisons do not isolate their causal effects. The authors acknowledge that accumulated iterations could have contributed.
+A production account makes a stronger assertion: because agents cannot change evaluator code, they cannot game the metric. Yet its own example describes higher weighted coherence from increasingly fine clusters, with deep levels collapsing into singletons and the hierarchy becoming unusable. Our interpretation is that this contradicts the broad prevention claim. Locking evaluator code protects the code; it does not ensure that higher scores mean useful output. The original training data are proprietary, which also limits reproduction of this account.
 
